adjudication.rationale:
In `PasskeysUsernamePasswordFormTest.reauthenticationOfUserWithoutPasskey()`, the diff introduces negative DOM checks implemented as:
```java
try {
  MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
  fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
} catch (Exception nsee) {
  // expected
}
```
`WebDriver.findElement(...)` does not return `null` when the element is absent; it throws (typically `NoSuchElementException`). Therefore the `assertThat(..., nullValue())` can never succeed and the `fail(...)` line is effectively unreachable in the intended “element absent” case. Worse, catching broad `Exception` will also swallow unrelated failures (driver/page load/stale element), allowing the test to pass for the wrong reason. The finding’s suggested fix (`Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, () -> findElement(...))`) aligns with how other tests in the diff previously asserted absence (e.g., earlier code used `Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, ...)`).
